A||||3||RZURCTN||HEI module meltdown?||||5
Z||000000||RZURCTN||10-08-1999||11:21 PM||||I recently tore apart my distibutor in hopes to find the reason that I was getting no spark or fuel.  In the process of testing everything I took the HEI module to an auto store to have it tested.  The guy plugged it in and it tested fine over and over again.  During the tests the module got pretty warm.  Is that supposed to happen?  A friend said that it shouldn't get warm at all.  At any rate, after many hours of chasing ghosts through the electrical system I found the cause of my lost spark and fuel.  The timing gear, in a fit of rage, self-shredded.  Who said that Fieros don't have personality?  Mine has one of those attention disorders.  The kind where if doesn't get enough, it MAKES you pay attention! Usually with an engine check light!||||reg||5
Z||000001||mshill||10-09-1999||01:42 AM||||The ignition module will definitely get hot while testing.  On the bottom of the module is a large metal surface.  It is a heat sink and you must have a generous amount of thermally conductive grease between it and the distributor base to dissipate the heat that the module generates.<P>When testing, the heat has nowhere to go.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-09-1999||02:32 PM||||if you need more heat sink grease you can get it at radio shack. Always apply fresh grease to clean surfaces. This will make your module stay much cooler.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||10-09-1999||03:33 PM||||I applied a generous amount of Die-electric grease when I reinstalled the module.  I was not having a problem getting spark with the dist. out.  ||||reg||14
